        {
          "text": "**Processing JSON for HTML**\n\nI’m handling a combined chunk of JSON data from 2001-5000, and even though it's lengthy, I don’t need all of it for the page implementation. Manually parsing can be a hassle, so I’m thinking of using bash to programmatically generate HTML with a Python script. I’ll write that script to read from the JSON file, parse it, and output to `docs/copilot-app-releases.html`. This approach is efficient and adheres to the \"DIRECT ACTION FIRST\" principle. After that, I'll ensure that the file is newly created by comparing it.",
          "type": "summary_text"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Generating Pull Request Script**\n\nI’ll need to create a git branch, commit the file, and then call `safeoutputs create_pull_request`. The next step involves running a bash command that executes a Python script. This script will read the JSON file, process releases, and write the resulting HTML to `docs/copilot-app-releases.html`. It's essential to ensure safe serialization and proper escaping while including specific features like deduplication by numeric ID, sorting by publication date, and extracting highlights. I want to implement regex to find relevant sections for fixes and summaries effectively.",
          "type": "summary_text"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Building HTML for Releases**\n\nIf there are no releases found, I’ll use \"None listed\". Next, I need to build the HTML page, including elements like the title, timestamp, release count, and filtering controls for all, stable, and prerelease versions. I’ll use inline CSS and vanilla JavaScript for this. I want to embed the releases data using a script tag, ensuring everything is properly safe-escaped.\n\nEach deduplicated release will have its own card, and after writing the file, I’ll summarize the count of lines and the newest version. Finally, I'll run git commands to create a branch, add and commit the file, and create a pull request, ensuring everything is configured accurately and follows the naming conventions.",
          "type": "summary_text"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Preparing Python Script for Release Processing**\n\nI’m using the UTC date to generate my branch name, which will look like \"refresh-release-20260912T194541Z.\" To set this up, I'll prepare a bash command with an initial wait time, possibly defaulting to 30 seconds, since operations are quick.\n\nNow, I'm getting into the Python script content. I'll import necessary modules like json, datetime, re, html, urllib, and os. The script will read a file path, deduplicate by ID, and sort based on the published or created dates. I’ll ensure safe URLs by checking that the scheme is 'https' and extract relevant sections from the body text using regex patterns for headings.",
          "type": "summary_text"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Implementing Release Highlights and Fixes**\n\nI'm implementing a simple approach for processing highlights and fixes. For highlights, I’ll consider headings like \"Highlights\", \"What's new\", and \"Added\". For fixes, I’ll look at options like \"Fixed\" and variations that might include headings formatted with '### Fixed'.\n\nNext, I'll extract bullet points, trimming to around six key items. I’ll build a list of release dictionaries for embedding and then write the HTML. It will use minimal CSS and JS to read JSON from a script tag, ensuring safe embedding by utilizing escape sequences. Finally, I’ll run the bash command to execute the Python script and follow up with the git steps for creating a pull request.",
          "type": "summary_text"
        }
      ],
